{"conversation_id": "conv_001", "status": "started", "response": "Imagine you have two magic coins that are “linked.” When you flip them, they can land on heads or tails. But these coins are special: if you look at one and see heads, you instantly know the other one is tails, even if it’s very far away, like on the other side of the Earth. Before you look, it’s like both coins are in a kind of “both heads and tails at the same time” state that we can’t see directly.

Quantum entanglement is like that, but with tiny particles instead of coins. When particles become entangled, their properties are connected in a deep way. If you measure one particle, you instantly know what the other one will show, no matter how far apart they are. It’s not that one sends a message to the other; it’s more like they are parts of the same invisible “team.”

This is very strange because it doesn’t match how everyday things behave. Even grown-up scientists like Einstein were puzzled by it. But lots of experiments show that entanglement is real. It’s so real that people are trying to use it to build super-secure communication and super-fast quantum computers, using this spooky connection between tiny particles.", "tokens_used": {"input": 1947, "output": 255, "cached": 0}}

